Canonical fill re-wraps prose greedily at the width budget, one layout
regardless of how the author broke their lines — author word-gap break points
are not preserved, only structural breaks (ChildrenMultiline) are. A cluster
bonded together by a significant space touching a non-word leaf (word+atom)
cannot be split at that joint, so a bonded cluster wider than the budget stays
on one long line rather than being forced open.

-- input.gsx --
package p

component LongProseWraps() {
	<p>Formatting decisions like this one are meant to read naturally: paragraphs of documentation prose should wrap at a sensible column width instead of overflowing off the right edge of the screen, and the formatter should do that work automatically so authors never have to think about where to break a line by hand.</p>
}

component AuthorWrapReflows() {
	<p>
		Hand-wrapped
		prose at an
		arbitrary short
		column count does
		not survive formatting: the printer discards every one of these
		author break points and re-wraps the whole paragraph at its own
		eighty column budget instead, exactly as if the source had been
		written on one long line to begin with.
	</p>
}

component BondClusterStaysLong() {
	<p>
		This sentence sits before a <code>reallyLongIdentifierNameThatByItselfAlreadyExceedsTheEightyColumnPrintWidthBudgetEntirely</code> and continues after it with more prose that wraps normally at the word gaps that follow.
	</p>
}
